          dealing: "Each player is dealt 12 cards, one at a time. The remaining 4 cards are placed face down in the center to form the 'Kitty'.",
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        rules: {
          objective: 'High bidder must win at least the number of tricks they bid (with their secret partner), or all 12 tricks (if bidding Amerikaner).',
          gameplay: "1. **Bidding**: Staring left of the dealer, players bid a number of tricks from 6 to 12. Each bid must be higher than the last. 2. **Amerikaner Bid**: The highest possible bid, an commitment to win all 12 tricks without a partner. 3. **The Kitty**: The high bidder takes the 4 kitty cards and discards any 4 cards face down. 4. **Calling the Partner**: The bidder leads the first card. The suit of this card becomes trumps. As they lead, they name a specific card of that suit (e.g., 'The Ace of Spades'). The player holding that card is the secret partner. 5. **Play**: Standard trick-taking rules. Highest card of led suit or highest trump wins the trick.",
          keyRules: [
            'Trump Suit: Always the suit of the very first lead made by the bidder.',
            'Grand: An optional bid in some variations where there is no trump suit.',
            'Secret Partner: Must play the called card to the first trick if possible.',
            'Amerikaner Win: If the bidder takes all 12 tricks alone, it is a massive score (+52).',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        strategy: {
          basic: 'If you have a strong suit but lack the top card (the Ace), bid high (8 or 9) and call the Ace of that suit to guarantee a powerful partner.',
          intermediate: "The Discard: When using the kitty, discard cards from a suit you are 'short' in. This allows you to trump into that suit early in the game.",
          advanced: "Defensive play for opponents: If you suspect a partner has been called, try to 'bleed' the bidder's trumps early to prevent them from cross-ruffing.",
          tips: [
            "Don't bid 12 unless you have a near-perfect hand. If you lose even one trick, you lose 12 points while your opponents gain from their captured tricks.",
            'Remember: the bidder MUST lead the trump suit first.',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
